{
  "meta": {
    "description": "Hand-curated, significance-based candidate list for the Milestones lane. Not a SPARQL dump like the sibling events-*.raw.json files — this lane's selection and categorization is curated by hand and verified against live Wikidata per item. Originally 121 entries across the first 10 categories; merged with a second AI-sourced candidate list whose QIDs were almost entirely wrong (83 of 94 candidate ids resolved to an unrelated Wikidata item) — every new entry was re-resolved to its correct QID by name, and any candidate with no matching Wikidata item or no live Wikidata date-property claim was dropped rather than guessed. That second list also introduced 10 new categories, folded into meta.categories below since the per-category color-legend cost was negligible. A third batch of 16 entries (of 20 candidates — 4 dropped for no live date-property claim: Germ theory of disease, CRISPR-Cas method, Discovery of nuclear fission, X-ray) was added the same way, this time hand-searched against Wikidata directly rather than AI-recalled. year/month are no longer curator-authored here — fetchMilestonesEnrichment resolves them live per QID (see queries/milestones-enrichment.ts's DATE_PROPERTIES), same sourcing Conflicts already uses for its dates. A 2026-08-13 recategorization pass: reassigned several items to a better-fitting existing category (e.g. railway/transit infrastructure moved from transportation to infrastructure, engines moved from transportation to energy-industry), dropped 10 lower-signal computing items from everyday-technology as noise, and split 5 pandemic/public-health items (Black Death, 1918 Spanish flu, COVID-19, US FDA, Hygiene) out of medicine-health into a new public-health category — the split was needed so the new 2-value MilestoneCategoryGroup grouping (science-innovation/social-culture, replacing the old 3-value one) could place them correctly (see shared-types). A same-day follow-up pass: moved archaeology-anthropology wholesale to social-culture; folded Great Irish Famine into public-health; split media-culture (Penny Black postage stamp, BBC Television Service launch, Facebook, Workers Leaving the Lumière Factory) out of communication; and merged architecture-design with infrastructure's single-structure items (Aqua Appia, Brooklyn Bridge, Channel Tunnel, Hoover Dam, Panama Canal, Suez Canal, The Iron Bridge, plus Escalator) into a new landmarks category — architecture-design retired, infrastructure now holds only its 5 transit-network items (First Transcontinental Railroad, High-speed rail, Interstate Highway System, London Underground, Stockton and Darlington Railway). A further same-day pass: renamed exploration to expedition, keeping its 7 journey/expedition items and adding The Travels of Marco Polo; moved Sextant to everyday-technology and Sputnik 1/Vostok 1/Voyager 1/International Space Station to transportation; moved View from the Window at Le Gras to media-culture and added Photography to communication; added Liverpool and Manchester Railway to infrastructure; and reassigned infrastructure's group from science-innovation to social-culture in its entirety, dropping the transit-vs-landmark distinction from the prior pass. A final same-day pass: added Vulcanization (energy-industry), Hand with Rings (media-culture), First solo nonstop transatlantic flight and 10 more expedition-category items (all hand-verified against live Wikidata after an initial candidate batch's QIDs turned out almost entirely wrong, the same failure mode as the second AI-sourced batch noted above); removed Green Revolution; and reassigned Voyager 1 and International Space Station out of transportation — Voyager 1 to expedition (alongside Voyager 2), International Space Station to landmarks (a structure/habitat, not a vehicle or journey). Removed Hand with Rings and First solo, nonstop transatlantic flight after the live fetch — both are correctly-identified Wikidata items but neither has an English Wikipedia article, so build-data would drop them regardless; left out entirely rather than kept as inert unpublishable entries.",
